  "name": "Question 20",
  "problem": "Which expression gives the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(\\frac{x}{4}-{60}^{\u2218}),$ where $x$ is measured in degrees? Assume that $n$ is an integer.",
  "worked_solution": "The vertical asymptotes of the function $y=\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x$ coincide with the zeros of $y=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x,$ and are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx=n\\cdot {180}^{\u2218},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes of $2\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(\\frac{x}{4}-{60}^{\u2218}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{4}-{60}^{\u2218}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}-{60}^{\u2218}=n\\cdot {180}^{\u2218}.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{4}-{60}^{\u2218} & =n\\cdot {180}^{\u2218} \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & ={60}^{\u2218}+n\\cdot {180}^{\u2218} \\\\ x & =4\\cdot {60}^{\u2218}+4\\cdot n\\cdot {180}^{\u2218} \\\\ x & ={240}^{\u2218}+n\\cdot {720}^{\u2218}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x={240}^{\u2218}+n\\cdot {720}^{\u2218},$ where $n$ is an integer.",
